    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-036 A1">Scope: the job contract. Implement IScheduledJob and stay host-neutral — no dependency on ScheduledJobHost, IBackgroundJobStore, ScheduledJobRegistry or IScheduledJobLease. One dispatch per schedule is the HOST's lease (task 103); write nothing for it.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-036 A1">Scope: every unit of work. Take an ATOMIC claim before the side effect and write a completion marker after it. IIdempotencyService as built is check-then-set and fails open (#984), so re-check under the claim exactly as GrantExpiryReminderJob does.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-036 A1">Scope: failure. THROW from ExecuteAsync only when a retry this tick could complete work the tick would otherwise lose; otherwise count the failure and complete. Emit ONE structured heartbeat per attempt including an attempt with nothing to do, carrying context.Attempt.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-036 A1.1">Scope: lease-store outage. A tick not dispatched must be caught up by the next tick. Reconciliation is naturally catch-up-safe because it is idempotent by construction — state that in the notes rather than adding a catch-up mechanism.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-052">Scope: placement. This runs IN the BFF on the in-process Spaarke.Scheduling host (BFF domain code, BFF identity, low volume), registered in ExternalAccessModule beside GrantExpiryReminderJob. State the Placement Justification in the PR.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-010">Scope: lifetime. AddScheduledJob registers the job Singleton. Resolve Scoped dependencies through IServiceScopeFactory.CreateScope() once per ExecuteAsync, mirroring GrantExpiryReminderJob — do not invent a new lifetime pattern.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: fail direction. A read that FAILS must never be recorded as "nothing to reconcile". Note the inversion deliberately: for this WRITER an empty-on-fault read is fail-SAFE (it writes nothing), which is the opposite of the read path's ISS-019 hazard — so the requirement here is that the run is recorded FAILED, not that it writes something.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-18 (D-2 part 3)">Scope: deployment. Rules R2 and R3 REMOVE access that exists today. Ship the job DISABLED, provide a report-only mode that counts and lists affected rows without writing, and record the before-state. Enabling it is an owner action, not part of this task.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11">Scope: machinery. Reuse IGenericEntityService for paged reads and BulkUpdateAsync for writes, ExternalGrantLifecycle for dates, IIdempotencyService for claims, and the existing scheduling host. No new package, client, store, scheduler or date constant.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11.5">Scope: cohesion. Three rules in one job is ONE responsibility — "make the row's own state the truth". Do not split it into three jobs, and do not accrete unrelated reconciliation into it later.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR. Placement Justification; publish size measured against a FRESH master worktree from a SHORT path with file counts compared on both sides (hazards 3 and 4); no new HIGH CVE.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests. KEEP path is tests/integration/auth/** for the authorization behaviour; the scheduling framework itself is already covered once in tests/unit/Spaarke.Scheduling.Tests — do not re-test the lease, slot guard or registration per job.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
