  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">R1 positive: an ACTIVE sprk_externalrecordaccess row with no sprk_expiresdate is stamped with ExternalGrantLifecycle.DefaultExpiry(TodayUtc) — the same value the endpoint writes, not a re-derived 90.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">R1 negative: a row that already carries any sprk_expiresdate — past, today or future — is left byte-for-byte unchanged.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">R2 positive: an ACTIVE grant row whose _sprk_organization_value references an INACTIVE sprk_organization is deactivated with BOTH statecode=1 and statuscode=2.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">R2 negative: a grant row whose organization is active, and a grant row with no organization lookup at all (a contact-keyed grant), are both left unchanged.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">R3 positive: an ACTIVE sprk_contactorganization row whose sprk_enddate has passed is deactivated with BOTH statecode=1 and statuscode=2.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">R3 negative: a junction row with a null sprk_enddate, and one whose sprk_enddate is today or in the future, are both left unchanged. Access holds THROUGH the end date, matching ExpiryPredicate's boundary.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A row whose statecode is NULL is treated as ACTIVE by every rule (ExternalGrantLifecycle.IsActive semantics), not skipped.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Report-only mode counts and lists every row each rule would change and writes NOTHING; it is the default until an owner enables writes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The job is registered via AddScheduledJob&lt;TJob&gt; with enabled:false, and no per-job bootstrap class is added.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Idempotent: a second run immediately after a successful first run finds nothing to change and reports zero changes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A run with nothing to do emits its heartbeat (carrying context.Attempt) and returns Success with ProcessedItems 0 — distinguishable in the logs from a run that died.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A read failure records the run as FAILED and writes nothing; it is never reported as "nothing to reconcile".</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A chunk whose BulkUpdateAsync transaction fails leaves every row in that chunk unchanged, is counted, and does not prevent later chunks from being applied.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A scan that exceeds the page ceiling reports the run TRUNCATED rather than silently reconciling a prefix.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Cancellation returns promptly, performs no further writes, and does not throw.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">ResultJson carries a per-rule breakdown (scanned / changed / failed / truncated) and Skipped is never set by the job.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A repo-wide grep shows exactly one definition of the 90-day default (ExternalGrantLifecycle.DefaultExpiryDays); this task adds none.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Perturbation: removing the per-chunk claim, or replacing BulkUpdateAsync with per-row PATCH, or dropping statuscode from the deactivation, each fails at least one test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; full suite green; ArchTests green; publish size reported against a fresh master with equal file counts on both sides and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in the task notes. Do not add near-identical happy-path variants.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Authored 2026-09-19 (session 17) via /task-create, consolidating three owner decisions into one mechanism.

SIBLINGS, not dependencies. Task 107 is D-1 option A — it inverts the READ default so a null sprk_expiresdate confers NOTHING. That closes the exposure immediately; THIS job cleans the data. They are complementary and neither blocks the other. Task 118 is D-1 option C (the role + UI-gate change). Tasks 109+110 own the READ guard for the junction (one read, two named sets: ConferringOrganizationIds date-bounded, WallSubjectOrganizationIds statecode-active) — this job is the WRITER half named in D-2 part 4. Once R3 has run, statecode alone is the truth and the read-time date comparison becomes belt-and-braces rather than load-bearing.

⚠️ ISS-026's READ guard is deliberately NOT in this task. It touches the same ExternalParticipationService query that 109+110 are already rewriting as a single snapshot; splitting one query's correctness across two tasks would re-introduce the two-snapshot hazard task 043 removed. Scoping judgment recorded 2026-09-19.

⚠️ THIS PROJECT HAS 19 PRIOR INSTANCES OF PREMISE ROT (`notes/task-accuracy-audit-2026-09-18.md:9`). Every load-bearing claim in the background section above was verified against source on 2026-09-19 with the file and line recorded so you can re-check it cheaply — but verify anyway, because the code has won every time. The two claims deliberately left UNVERIFIED are named in the escalation triggers, and they are unverified because the Dataverse MCP server was down (CONNECTION_CLOSED), not because nobody looked.

Register: notes/defer-issues.md § ISS-026 (#1006) and § ISS-020 (#999). Decisions: notes/owner-decision-brief-2026-09-18.md § Outcomes rows D-1, D-2, D-9/ISS-026.</notes>

